            <h2 class="section-title">Install These Tools Before You Start Vibe Coding</h2>
            <p class="section-subtitle">Start with the four essentials. Install stack-specific tools only after the project planner recommends a technology stack—beginners do not need to install everything on this page.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="section-outcome"><b>BEGINNER GOAL</b><span>Editor + Git + account + one runtime, verified and ready</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="setup-callout">
          <strong>Use this order</strong>
          <div><span>1</span> Install the essentials</div>
          <div><span>2</span> Restart your computer</div>
          <div><span>3</span> Run the verification commands</div>
          <div><span>4</span> Complete the planner</div>
          <div><span>5</span> Install only the selected stack</div>
        </div>

        <div class="already-have">
          <div><span>Already included</span><b>Web browser</b><small>Use an updated Chrome, Edge, Firefox, or Safari and learn to open its Developer Tools.</small></div>
          <div><span>Already included</span><b>Terminal</b><small>Use PowerShell on Windows or Terminal on macOS/Linux. You do not need to download another terminal.</small></div>
          <div><span>Important</span><b>Do not install everything</b><small>The planner chooses the runtime, database, and deployment tools appropriate for the client project.</small></div>
        </div>

        <div class="setup-stages">
        <details class="setup-stage">
          <summary>
            <span>Step 1</span>
            <div><b>Install the four essentials</b><small>VS Code, Git, GitHub, and one AI assistant</small></div>
            <i>Open instructions</i>
          </summary>
          <div class="setup-stage-content">
        <div class="tool-section-head">
          <div><span class="tool-level tool-level--required">Install first</span><h3>The four essentials for everyone</h3></div>
          <p>These are enough to plan a project, edit files, track changes, and work with an AI coding assistant.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-install-grid">
          <article class="install-card">
            <div class="install-card-top"><span class="install-number">01</span><span class="tool-level tool-level--required">Required</span></div>
            <h3>Visual Studio Code</h3>
            <p>Your code editor. Open the entire project folder in it so the AI can understand the repository—not just one file.</p>
            <div class="install-choice"><b>Choose:</b> Stable release for your operating system.</div>
            <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official download ↗</a>
            <code>code --version</code>
            <small>If the command fails on macOS, open VS Code’s Command Palette and install the <b>code</b> command in PATH.</small>
          </article>
          <article class="install-card">
            <div class="install-card-top"><span class="install-number">02</span><span class="tool-level tool-level--required">Required</span></div>
            <h3>Git</h3>
            <p>Git saves checkpoints of your work. It lets you review AI changes and return to an earlier working version.</p>
            <div class="install-choice"><b>Windows:</b> Keep the installer defaults. macOS/Linux may already include Git.</div>
            <a href="https://git-scm.com/downloads/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official download ↗</a>
            <code>git --version</code>
            <small>Git is the tool. GitHub is the online service where you can store a private repository.</small>
          </article>
          <article class="install-card">
            <div class="install-card-top"><span class="install-number">03</span><span class="tool-level tool-level--account">Account</span></div>
            <h3>GitHub account</h3>
            <p>Create a free account, verify your email, and enable two-factor authentication. Client repositories should be private and client-owned.</p>
            <div class="install-choice"><b>No download:</b> This is an online account.</div>
            <a href="https://github.com/signup" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Create an account ↗</a>
            <a class="install-sub-link" href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/securing-your-account-with-two-factor-authentication-2fa/configuring-two-factor-authentication" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Set up 2FA ↗</a>
            <small>Save recovery codes in a password manager. Never send repository passwords through chat.</small>
          </article>
          <article class="install-card">
            <div class="install-card-top"><span class="install-number">04</span><span class="tool-level tool-level--required">Required</span></div>
            <h3>AI coding assistant</h3>
            <p>Choose one repository-aware assistant: Codex, Cursor, Claude Code, GitHub Copilot, or another tool supported by your workflow.</p>
            <div class="install-choice"><b>Choose one:</b> You do not need several AI tools.</div>
            <a href="#planner">Choose inside the planner ↓</a>
            <small>Never paste client secrets, passwords, private customer records, or production database exports into an AI chat.</small>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="os-quick-guides">
          <details open>
            <summary><b>Windows beginner steps</b><span>Use .exe or .msi installers</span></summary>
            <ol><li>Download the VS Code <b>User Installer</b> and run it.</li><li>Install Git for Windows and keep the recommended defaults.</li><li>Restart Windows or close and reopen PowerShell.</li><li>Run <code>code --version</code> and <code>git --version</code>.</li><li>After planning, install only the selected runtime—usually the Node.js LTS Windows installer for a JavaScript project.</li></ol>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><b>macOS beginner steps</b><span>Use .dmg or .pkg installers</span></summary>
            <ol><li>Open the VS Code .dmg and drag the application into Applications.</li><li>In VS Code, install the <code>code</code> command through the Command Palette.</li><li>Open Terminal and run <code>git --version</code>; macOS will guide you if developer tools are needed.</li><li>After planning, install only the selected runtime.</li></ol>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><b>Linux beginner steps</b><span>Use your distribution’s official package</span></summary>
            <ol><li>Follow the official VS Code instructions for your distribution’s .deb, .rpm, Snap, or repository package.</li><li>Install Git with the distribution package manager.</li><li>Close and reopen the terminal, then verify Git and VS Code.</li><li>Follow the approved project setup for the runtime; do not copy random installation commands from blogs.</li></ol>
          </details>
        </div>
          </div>
        </details>

