          <p>A runtime executes the application. Do not install all three. Use the exact version written in the generated architecture.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="runtime-grid">
          <article>
            <div><span>Most JavaScript websites</span><h3>Node.js LTS</h3></div>
            <p>For React, Next.js, Vue, Vite, and Node servers. npm is included.</p>
            <a href="https://nodejs.org/en/download" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official download ↗</a>
            <pre><code>node --version
npm --version</code></pre>
          </article>
          <article>
            <div><span>Python projects only</span><h3>Python</h3></div>
            <p>For Django, Flask, FastAPI, scripts, or when the approved stack requires Python.</p>
            <a href="https://www.python.org/downloads/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official download ↗</a>
            <pre><code>python --version
pip --version</code></pre>
          </article>
          <article>
            <div><span>PHP projects only</span><h3>PHP + Composer</h3></div>
            <p>For Laravel, WordPress, or PHP hosting. Install PHP first, then Composer.</p>
            <a href="https://www.php.net/downloads.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PHP download ↗</a>
            <a class="install-sub-link" href="https://getcomposer.org/download/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Composer download ↗</a>
            <pre><code>php --version
composer --version</code></pre>
          </article>
        </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="setup-stage">
          <summary>
            <span>Step 3</span>
            <div><b>Add optional project tools</b><small>Database, API testing, containers, and account security</small></div>
            <i>Open only if needed</i>
          </summary>
          <div class="setup-stage-content">
        <div class="tool-section-head">
          <div><span class="tool-level tool-level--optional">Only when required</span><h3>Database, testing, and deployment helpers</h3></div>
          <p>Ask the planner and your approved architecture before installing these. A hosted service may replace a local database or container tool.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="optional-tools">
          <details>
            <summary><span>Database</span><b>PostgreSQL or MySQL</b><small>Only when the project stores structured data locally</small></summary>
            <div><p>Choose the database named by the approved architecture. Do not install both. PostgreSQL installers commonly include pgAdmin; MySQL offers MySQL Workbench.</p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/download/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PostgreSQL ↗</a><a href="https://dev.mysql.com/downloads/installer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MySQL ↗</a></div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><span>API testing</span><b>Bruno or Postman</b><small>Helpful when the system has a backend API</small></summary>
            <div><p>Use one API client to send requests and inspect responses. This is useful, but not required for a basic static website.</p><a href="https://www.usebruno.com/downloads" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bruno ↗</a><a href="https://www.postman.com/downloads/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Postman ↗</a></div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><span>Containers</span><b>Docker Desktop</b><small>Install only when the setup guide uses containers</small></summary>
            <div><p>Docker can make databases and services repeatable, but it adds complexity and may require virtualization. Beginners can skip it unless the selected project explicitly uses Docker.</p><a href="https://docs.docker.com/desktop/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Docker Desktop ↗</a></div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><span>Security</span><b>Password manager + authenticator</b><small>Required before handling client accounts</small></summary>
            <div><p>Store recovery codes and unique credentials in an approved password manager. Enable MFA on GitHub, hosting, domain, email, database, and payment accounts.</p><a href="https://bitwarden.com/download/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bitwarden ↗</a><a href="https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/1066447" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Authenticator guidance ↗</a></div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><span>Editor add-ons</span><b>VS Code extensions</b><small>Install only what the chosen stack documents</small></summary>
            <div><p>VS Code already includes Git, debugging basics, a terminal, and common language support. Add a formatter, linter, or framework extension only when the approved repository recommends it. Check the publisher before installing.</p><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/configure/extensions/extension-marketplace" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Extension safety guide ↗</a></div>
          </details>
        </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="setup-stage">
          <summary>
            <span>Step 4</span>
            <div><b>Verify the installation</b><small>Run a few safe version commands, then continue to planning</small></div>
            <i>Show ready check</i>
          </summary>
          <div class="setup-stage-content">
        <div class="setup-finish">
          <div><span>Ready check</span><h3>Open a terminal and run these commands</h3><p>A version number means the tool is available. “Command not found” usually means the installation did not finish or the terminal needs to be reopened.</p></div>
          <pre><code>git --version
code --version

# Run only the runtime you selected:
node --version
python --version
php --version</code></pre>
          <a class="btn-sm-primary" href="#planner">Tools ready—plan my project →</a>
        </div>
          </div>
        </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section scrollspy-section" id="planner" data-nav="Plan">
        <div class="section-header focused-section-head">
          <div>
            <div class="section-tag">Step 2 · Plan</div>
            <h2 class="section-title">Describe the Whole System Before Generating Code</h2>
            <p class="section-subtitle">The questions are grouped into small steps. Answer what you know, mark uncertain decisions, and verify the final documents with the client.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="section-outcome"><b>OUTPUT</b><span>Context kit + master prompt + build plan + fixed scaffold</span></div>
        </div>
        <div id="doc-generator-container"></div>
      </section>

      <section class="section scrollspy-section" id="build" data-nav="Build">
        <div class="section-header focused-section-head">
          <div>
            <div class="section-tag">Step 3 · Build</div>
            <h2 class="section-title">Vibe Code Through Small, Complete Features</h2>
            <p class="section-subtitle">Do not ask AI to build the entire system. Repeat this loop for every approved feature until the MVP is complete.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="section-outcome"><b>RULE</b><span>One outcome, relevant context, real verification</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="learning-modules">
        <details class="learning-module">
          <summary><span>01</span><div><b>Follow the six-step build loop</b><small>Use this for every feature</small></div><i>Show steps</i></summary>
          <div class="learning-module-content">
        <div class="build-loop">
          <article><b>01</b><div><h3>Choose one outcome</h3><p>Example: “Staff can approve a pending request.” Define what the user should observe when it works.</p></div></article>
          <article><b>02</b><div><h3>Give relevant context</h3><p>Attach `RULES.md`, the relevant PRD and schema sections, plus only the code paths involved.</p></div></article>
          <article><b>03</b><div><h3>Ask for a plan</h3><p>Have the AI inspect the repository, name assumptions, and identify the smallest vertical slice before editing.</p></div></article>
          <article><b>04</b><div><h3>Implement the slice</h3><p>Include interface, validation, business rule, storage, permissions, error states, and tests when applicable.</p></div></article>
          <article><b>05</b><div><h3>Run and verify</h3><p>Require the exact checks it ran. Test the success path, failure path, forbidden role, and important edge cases.</p></div></article>
          <article><b>06</b><div><h3>Record and continue</h3><p>Update context and handoff notes, commit the working slice, then start the next focused session.</p></div></article>
        </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="learning-module">
          <summary><span>02</span><div><b>Write a clear AI prompt</b><small>Context + Goal + Behavior + Limits + Done</small></div><i>Show recipe</i></summary>
          <div class="learning-module-content">
        <div class="prompt-formula">
          <div class="prompt-formula-intro">
            <span>Prompt recipe</span>
            <h3>Context + Goal + Behavior + Limits + Done</h3>
            <p>If a prompt contains these five ingredients, the AI has fewer reasons to guess or change unrelated code.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="prompt-formula-parts">
            <div><b>01</b><strong>Context</strong><span>Which docs and files matter?</span></div>
            <div><b>02</b><strong>Goal</strong><span>Why does the user need it?</span></div>
            <div><b>03</b><strong>Behavior</strong><span>What happens step by step?</span></div>
            <div><b>04</b><strong>Limits</strong><span>What must remain unchanged?</span></div>
            <div><b>05</b><strong>Done</strong><span>What checks must pass?</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="learning-module">
          <summary><span>03</span><div><b>Give AI only the relevant context</b><small>Know which Markdown file to include</small></div><i>Show context guide</i></summary>
          <div class="learning-module-content">
        <div class="context-routing">
          <div class="context-routing-intro">
            <span class="section-tag">Save tokens with context routing</span>
            <h3>Give the AI only what the current task needs</h3>
            <p>The full context kit stays in the repository. Individual sessions reference the relevant documents and code paths.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="context-routing-list">
            <div><code>PRD.md</code><span>Scope, users, behavior, and acceptance</span></div>
            <div><code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code><span>Layers, data flow, and file placement</span></div>
            <div><code>SCHEMA.md</code><span>Data, APIs, permissions, and validation</span></div>
            <div><code>DESIGN.md</code><span>Screens, components, states, and accessibility</span></div>
            <div><code>RULES.md</code><span>Always include for implementation work</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="learning-module">
          <summary><span>04</span><div><b>Compare a vague and a useful prompt</b><small>See a complete approval-feature example</small></div><i>Show example</i></summary>
          <div class="learning-module-content">
        <div class="prompt-comparison">
          <div class="prompt-comparison-head">
            <div><span class="section-tag">Good vs. vague</span><h3>Describe observable behavior, not just a feature name</h3></div>
            <p>Example task: add approval to a staff dashboard.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="prompt-comparison-grid">
            <article class="prompt-example prompt-example--bad">
              <span>Vague</span>
              <blockquote>“Add an approval feature and make it work.”</blockquote>
              <ul><li>No relevant files</li><li>No permissions</li><li>No failure behavior</li><li>No completion checks</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="prompt-example prompt-example--good">
              <span>Clear</span>
              <blockquote>“Read RULES.md, the approval requirements in PRD.md, SCHEMA.md, and the request module. Staff may approve pending requests assigned to them; clients cannot approve. Save the approver and time, prevent duplicate approval, show success and error states, and add tests for allowed, forbidden, and already-approved cases. Inspect first, propose a plan, and change only relevant files.”</blockquote>
              <ul><li>Routes the right context</li><li>Defines behavior and permissions</li><li>Covers edge and error states</li><li>Requires tests and scope control</li></ul>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
          </div>
        </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section scrollspy-section" id="structure" data-nav="Structure">
        <div class="section-header focused-section-head">
          <div>
            <div class="section-tag">Step 4 · Structure</div>
            <h2 class="section-title">Use This Folder Contract for Every Project</h2>
            <p class="section-subtitle">Framework-specific filenames may change, but these responsibilities stay in familiar places. The generated prompt instructs AI to follow this structure.</p>
          </div>
          <button class="btn-secondary" id="copy-tree-btn">${'Copy folder tree'}</button>
        </div>
        <div class="folder-explorer-layout">
          <div class="tree-card" id="tree-container"></div>
          <div class="tree-detail-box">
            <div class="detail-header"><span class="detail-icon">i</span><span class="detail-name" id="tree-detail-name">project-root</span></div>
            <p class="detail-desc" id="tree-detail-desc">Select a folder or file to understand its single responsibility.</p>
            <div class="structure-rules">
              <h3>Rules the AI must follow</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Pages compose components; they do not contain every responsibility.</li>
                <li>Client API calls live in `client/src/services/`.</li>
                <li>Business logic lives in `server/src/services/`.</li>
                <li>Roles, statuses, and fixed values live in `constants/`.</li>
                <li>Every database change gets a migration and schema update.</li>
                <li>Project facts stay in `context/`; session notes stay in `docs/handoffs/`.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section scrollspy-section" id="launch" data-nav="Launch">
        <div class="section-header focused-section-head">
          <div>
            <div class="section-tag">Step 5 · Launch</div>
            <h2 class="section-title">Do Not Deploy Until These Gates Pass</h2>
            <p class="section-subtitle">AI can help perform the checks, but a human must verify sensitive data, permissions, backups, client acceptance, and production access.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="section-outcome"><b>FINISH</b><span>Tested release + rollback plan + client handover</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="launch-gates">
          <article><span>Gate 01</span><h3>Requirements accepted</h3><ul><li>MVP and non-goals approved</li><li>Every feature has acceptance checks</li><li>Roles and permissions confirmed</li><li>Open assumptions resolved</li></ul></article>
          <article><span>Gate 02</span><h3>System verified</h3><ul><li>Success and failure paths tested</li><li>Forbidden access tested</li><li>Responsive and accessible</li><li>No critical errors or fake fallbacks</li></ul></article>
          <article><span>Gate 03</span><h3>Production prepared</h3><ul><li>Secrets stored outside code</li><li>Migration and backup tested</li><li>Logs contain no sensitive data</li><li>Monitoring and rollback documented</li></ul></article>
          <article><span>Gate 04</span><h3>Client handed over</h3><ul><li>Staging accepted in writing</li><li>Admin and user guides delivered</li><li>Credential ownership transferred</li><li>Support period and backlog recorded</li></ul></article>
        </div>
        <div class="final-reminder">
          <strong>Production rule</strong>
          <p>Vibe code the prototype. Review, test, secure, back up, and obtain client approval before shipping the production system.</p>
          <a href="#planner">Plan another system ↑</a>
        </div>

        <details class="learning-module roadmap-module">
          <summary><span>Optional</span><div><b>Explore future platform capabilities</b><small>Ideas for team sync, approvals, estimates, and deployment adapters</small></div><i>Show roadmap</i></summary>
          <div class="learning-module-content">
        <div class="feature-roadmap">
